Creating a fake ransomware notification for a cybersecurity training exercise requires a balance between authenticity and safety. Here's a step-by-step guide to designing one using HTML and CSS:

### Step 1: HTML Structure
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Ransomware Notification</title>
    <style>
        body {
            background-color: #1a1a1a;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            min-height: 100vh;
            margin: 0;
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
        }

        .notification {
            background-color: #ffd700;
            padding: 30px;
            border-radius: 10px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);
            text-align: center;
            width: 80%;
            max-width: 500px;
        }

        .warning-icon {
            font-size: 40px;
            color: #cc0000;
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }

        .message {
            font-size: 24px;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
            font-weight: bold;
        }

        .file-list {
            margin: 15px 0;
            font-size: 16px;
        }

        .countdown {
            font-size: 18px;
            margin: 15px 0;
        }

        .decrypt-button {
            background-color: #3498db;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            padding: 10px 25px;
            border-radius: 5px;
            font-size: 16px;
            cursor: pointer;
            margin-top: 15px;
            transition: background-color 0.3s;
        }

        .decrypt-button:hover {
            background-color: #2980b9;
        }

        .timer {
            font-size: 14px;
            color: #666;
            margin-top: 5px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="notification">
        <div class="warning-icon">⚠️</div>
        <div class="message">Your Files Are Encrypted</div>
        <div class="file-list">
            • Files Encrypted:
            <br>
            • Documents
            • Images
            • Database
            • Backup Files
        </div>
        <div class="countdown">
            Time Until Decryption: 24:00:00
        </div>
        <button class="decrypt-button">Decrypt Now</button>
        <div class="timer">This message will expire in 24 hours</div>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

### Step 2: CSS Styling
- **Background Color**: Use a dark background to make the notification stand out.
- **Notification Box**: Create a centered box with a yellow background to mimic a warning dialog.
- **Text Size**: Use large, bold text for the main message to grab attention.
- **Warning Symbol**: Include a red warning symbol (like a triangle or lock icon) to indicate urgency.
- **Button**: Make the "Decrypt Now" button prominent with a distinct color and hover effect.
- **Countdown Timer**: Add a timer to increase urgency.

### Step 3: Features
- **Red Warning Symbol**: Use an SVG or Unicode character for a professional look.
- **File List**: Include sample encrypted files to simulate real content.
- **Countdown Timer**: Use JavaScript to update the timer every second.
- **Decryption Button**: Include a button with hover effects for interactivity.
- **Support Information**: Add a footer with contact information for help.

### Step 4: Usage
- **Display**: Present the notification on a computer screen during your training session.
- **Simulation**: Test the notification to ensure it appears and closes properly.
- **Training Use**: Explain to participants how to recognize and handle such notifications.

### Notes:
- Always remind participants that this is a simulation and no actual encryption is happening.
- Use this for training purposes only in a controlled environment.
- Customize the content, colors, and styling to match your organization's needs.

This fake ransomware notification should effectively simulate a real-life scenario while maintaining a safe environment for training purposes.<｜end▁of▁sentence｜>

