Creating a fake ransomware notification for a cybersecurity training exercise can be a useful tool for educating users about the potential risks of ransomware. Below is a step-by-step explanation of how you might design and create one:

### Step 1: Design the Notification
The notification should be visually urgent and include key elements that ransomware messages typically have:
- **Warning Symbol**: Use a red warning triangle or a padlock icon with a red X over it.
- **Message**: Clearly state that the user's data has been encrypted.
- **Ransom Amount**: Include a specific amount (e.g., $10,000).
- **Deadline**: Show a countdown timer (e.g., 24 hours).
- **Call to Action**: A button to "Pay Now" or "Decrypt Data."
- **Background**: Use a dark background with a black and red color scheme.

### Step 2: Create the HTML Structure
Here's an example HTML structure:

```html
<div class="ransomware-notification">
  <div class="warning-icon"></div>
  <div class="message">
    <h2>Your Data Has Been Encrypted</h2>
    <p>Your files are currently encrypted. You have 24 hours to pay the ransom or your data will be lost forever.</p>
    <div class="ransom-amount">$10,000</div>
    <div class="countdown">Time Left: 24:00:00</div>
    <button class="pay-button">Pay Now</button>
  </div>
  <div class="additional-info">
    <h3>Affected Files:</h3>
    <p>Documents, photos, backups, and other important files are encrypted.</p>
    <h3>Important Note:</h3>
    <p>Do not click on any suspicious links or attempt to decrypt data on your own. Contact us with the ransom payment details.</p>
    <button class="close-button">Close</button>
  </div>
</div>
```

### Step 3: Add CSS Styling
Use CSS to style the notification to make it look professional and urgent:

```css
.ransomware-notification {
  position: fixed;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  background: #000;
  padding: 20px;
  border-radius: 10px;
  box-shadow: 0 0 20px #ff0000;
  z-index: 1000;
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

.warning-icon {
  width: 40px;
  height: 40px;
  background: #ff0000;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  margin-bottom: 20px;
}

.warning-icon::before {
  content: "🔴";
  color: white;
  font-size: 24px;
}

.message {
  color: white;
  font-size: 18px;
  line-height: 1.5;
}

.ransom-amount {
  color: #ff0000;
  font-size: 24px;
  font-weight: bold;
  margin: 20px 0;
}

.countdown {
  color: #888;
  font-size: 14px;
  margin: 10px 0;
}

.pay-button {
  background: #ff6b00;
  color: white;
  border: none;
  padding: 10px 20px;
  border-radius: 5px;
  cursor: pointer;
  font-weight: bold;
  margin: 10px 0;
  transition: background 0.3s;
}

.pay-button:hover {
  background: #ff5500;
}

.close-button {
  background: #ff0000;
  color: white;
  border: none;
  padding: 8px 16px;
  border-radius: 4px;
  cursor: pointer;
  margin-top: 10px;
  transition: background 0.3s;
}

.close-button:hover {
  background: #cc0000;
}

@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .ransomware-notification {
    padding: 15px;
    padding-top: 20px;
    font-size: 16px;
  }
}
```

### Step 4: Add Interactivity
Use JavaScript to make the notification interactive:

```javascript
// Enlarge on click
document.querySelector('.ransomware-notification').addEventListener('click', function() {
  this.style.width = '800px';
  this.style.height = '600px';
});

// Countdown timer
let timeLeft = 24 * 60 * 60; // 24 hours in seconds
let timer = setInterval(function() {
  timeLeft--;
  document.querySelector('.countdown').textContent = 
    'Time Left: ' + timeLeft;
  if (timeLeft <= 0) {
    clearInterval(timer);
    document.querySelector('.countdown').textContent = 'Time Expired';
  }
}, 1000);
```

### Step 5: Test and Customize
- **Test the notification** on different screen sizes to ensure it's responsive.
- **Customize the colors, text, and icons** to match your organization's branding or preferences.
- **Add your logo** or organization name to the notification.
- **Include specific file types** affected by the ransomware to make it more realistic.

### Final Notes:
- **Ensure it's clear that this is a simulation** and not a real ransomware attack.
- **Use it only for training purposes** and delete it after the exercise.
- **Warn users not to click on suspicious links or pay ransom** in real scenarios.

This fake ransomware notification can help train users to recognize and respond appropriately to real-world ransomware threats.<｜end▁of▁sentence｜>

